KG42.register("edges-ecd187c74fec660f4050dec0",{"id":"S-809135306b16ee45","source":"COMP-486b93e7e3eb4336","type":"limits","target":"COMP-b3f649e90c1f17b9","evidence_class":"S","scientific_acceptance":false,"relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"paper_ids":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation"],"assertions":[{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::E::v141-c-R002-E003","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002","record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R002-E003","source":"matching.counterparties","target":"bidding.supply","type":"limits","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"evidence_class":"S","provenance_gate":"direct_C_edge_with_constraint_and_source_excerpt","scientific_acceptance":false,"accepted_constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C003"],"held_constraint_ids":[],"semantic_review_status":"supported","source":"COMP-486b93e7e3eb4336","target":"COMP-b3f649e90c1f17b9","source_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::V::matching.counterparties","target_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::V::bidding.supply","source_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","target_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::bidding.supply","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"active":true,"source_signature":"6f81500b8b000ee8925fbfbbf2bea8d508b84526de14ff228c877e723adc361d","relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"source_science":{"mechanism_id":"v141-M01","source_definition":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents. A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price. Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners. The bilateral trade power from agent n to agent m must be zero when m is outside n’s partnership set. Scope: Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n’s trading partnership set. Partnership sets specify possible trades and do not themselves enforce market participation. Scope: Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation. Partnership sets could be dynamically adapted in response to congestion; this is a proposed possibility rather than an asserted implemented procedure. Scope: Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. Condition: Congestion occurs.","target_definition":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power. The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set. The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a). When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set. Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set.","expression":"p_{nm}=0\\quad\\text{if }m\\notin\\omega_n","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"]}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"source_record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R002-E003","source":"matching.counterparties","target":"bidding.supply","type":"limits","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"paper_scope_note":"The bilateral negotiated price update and the exogenous network fee signal are separately limited in meaning; partner-set constraints, incentive intent, and ADMM computational dependencies cannot be transmitted in a mixed way."}}],"assertion_ids":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::E::v141-c-R002-E003"]});
